0 < 2 # Fold vs serialize at width 8 — the measurement 0.3.0 still owed
1 < 2 
2 < 2 **Status:** pre-registered protocol. Predictions committed BEFORE either cell ran
3 < 2 (sitting 2 of the merge-frontier sprint; #360's Amendment-9 comment names this the
4 < 2 standing measurement owed by 0.3.0). The 2026-08-30 A/B re-established fold's advantage
5 < 2 on the One Driver engine, but its widest contended wave was **4** (`contend-big`);
6 < 2 measured dispatch width is flat to 12 and the default is 8 (`2026-08-28-wave-width.md`).
7 < 2 Whether same-file overlap pays **at width 8** has never been measured. This is that cell.
8 < 2 
9 < 2 ## Method
10 < 2 
11 < 2 - **Fixture:** `evals/fixtures/contend-wide` — eight independent implementation tasks all
12 < 2   editing `clitool/cli.py` (own parser line, own helper, own line in `main`), suite
13 < 2   acceptance. Compiled arm identity verified before running: fold = **1 wave of 8**,
14 < 2   serialize = **8 single-task waves**.
15 < 2 - **Rig:** `evals/ab_runner.py`, both arms, serial, local (laptop subscription), same as
16 < 2   the 2026-08-30 twelve-cell run. Engine ref = current main. Rows append to
17 < 2   `evals/results/runs.jsonl`.
18 < 2 - **Order:** runs AFTER the #454 concurrent-drain arms, so the `/usage` reads of that
19 < 2   measurement are not contaminated by local cells.
20 < 2 
21 < 2 ## Pre-registered predictions
22 < 2 
23 < 2 1. **Wall ratio (fold/serialize) < 0.5**, point prediction **0.30–0.45** — extrapolating
24 < 2    contend-big's 0.358 at width 4: twice the serialized waves, but smaller per-task work,
25 < 2    so per-wave fixed cost (suite pass, wave choreography) dominates the serialize arm.
26 < 2 2. **Arm identity holds:** the fold cell folds one 8-way contended wave; the serialize
27 < 2    cell runs 8 waves. If either compiles differently, the cell is invalid, not evidence.
28 < 2 3. **Token ratio (fold/serialize) between 0.8 and 1.4** — the 08-30 run put the fold
29 < 2    token penalty at 1.09× aggregate, inverting to 0.93× on the biggest serialize chain.
30 < 2 4. **Noise floor:** ±5% (established by the 08-30 identical-shape fixtures). A wall ratio
31 < 2    above 0.9 would be inconclusive rather than a refutation.
32 < 2 
33 < 2 ## Results (cells ran 2026-09-01 00:32–00:52 UTC, serial, laptop, engine `dbf6ec1`)
34 < 2 
35 < 2 **The predicted band missed. At width 8 under total contention, fold's wall advantage
36 < 2 vanishes — and the event log says exactly why.**
37 < 2 
38 < 2 | arm | verdict | wall | waves | output tok | cost |
39 < 2 |---|---|---|---|---|---|
40 < 2 | fold | approved | 579.2s | 1 × 8 | 78,692 | $7.81 |
41 < 2 | serialize | approved | 586.2s | 8 × 1 | 42,530 | $4.40 |
42 < 2 
43 < 2 - **Wall ratio 0.988** — inside the ±5% noise floor. Per the pre-registered reading rule
44 < 2   (prediction 4), that is *inconclusive on wall*, not a measured serialize win; but the
45 < 2   predicted 0.30–0.45 band is cleanly missed.
46 < 2 - **Token prediction REFUTED:** fold/serialize output-token ratio **1.85** (predicted
47 < 2   0.8–1.4). Fold paid nearly double.
48 < 2 - **Arm identity held** (prediction 2): 1 wave of 8 vs 8 waves of 1; both cells gate-green.
49 < 2 
50 < 2 ## The mechanism (from `events.jsonl`, per-role wall)
51 < 2 
52 < 2 | role | fold | serialize |
53 < 2 |---|---|---|
54 < 2 | implementers | 8 workers, 295s total (parallel) | 8 workers, 286s total (sequential) |
55 < 2 | **resolvers** | **7 dispatches, 419s (sequential)** | 0 |
56 < 2 | reviews | 8, 153s | 8, 178s |
57 < 2 | integration | 79s | 114s |
58 < 2 
59 < 2 Fold parallelized ~286s of sequential implementation — and then handed back **419s of
60 < 2 sequential resolver chain**, because the kernel contract dispatches one resolver at a time
61 < 2 and an 8-way single-file contention produced 7 of them. **The serialization did not
62 < 2 disappear; it moved from the wave axis to the resolver axis, and at this task size the
63 < 2 resolver chain is longer than the implementation chain it replaced.**
64 < 2 
65 < 2 This reconciles cleanly with the width-4 lineage instead of contradicting it: contend-big's
66 < 2 0.358× win came from *large* tasks (minutes of implementation serialized away, few
67 < 2 resolutions); contend-wide's tasks are small and maximally contended. **Fold's advantage
68 < 2 is not monotonic in width — it scales with (serial implementation time saved) minus
69 < 2 (resolver-chain length), and the second term grows with the number of tasks contending on
70 < 2 one file.**
71 < 2 
72 < 2 ## Two confounds, stated
73 < 2 
74 < 2 1. **The suite here is ~free** (the fixture's tests run in seconds), so serialize's 7 extra
75 < 2    waves paid almost no suite tax. On the fleet, where a suite pass is ~90s, those waves
76 < 2    would cost serialize ~10 more minutes — the #443 billing argument for fold survives
77 < 2    wherever suite passes are expensive; it just didn't bite in this local cell.
78 < 2 2. **Total contention is the worst case for fold by construction** — every task edits the
79 < 2    one file. Real plans mix contended and clean files; the 08-30 aggregate (0.594×) remains
80 < 2    the representative number.
81 < 2 
82 < 2 ## Decision returned
83 < 2 
84 < 2 **The default does not change** — fold showed no wall or quality regression even in its
85 < 2 worst case, and its suite-pass economics dominate on real substrates. What changes is the
86 < 2 efficiency *thesis*: "fold pays more the wider the wave" is now bounded. The measured
87 < 2 frontier for kernel work (#360) is the **resolver chain**: at high contention width it is
88 < 2 the new serial bottleneck, and hunk-group-parallel resolution (or resolver batching) is
89 < 2 the lever that would make width-8 contention actually pay. Token cost of extreme-width
90 < 2 folding (1.85×) goes on the record as the price of that resolver chain.
91 < 2 